Short answer: those middle two digits are the SSN’s “group number.” Historically they were an administrative code SSA used when assigning numbers, but they don’t have any meaningful personal information (like age or exact location) about you.

Details, briefly:
- SSN format = AAA‑GG‑SSSS (Area – Group – Serial).
  - Area (first three digits): originally indicated the state or territory where the application was filed.
  - Group (middle two digits): the “group number.” It was used to break an area’s block of numbers into smaller batches for processing and fraud-detection purposes. Group numbers run 01–99; 00 is not valid.
  - Serial (last four digits): a sequence number 0001–9999 (0000 is not valid).
- SSA changed to randomized assignment on June 25, 2011 (SSN Randomization). Since then, area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate geography or timing — they’re basically part of a randomized number now.
- Some blocks are not/weren’t used (e.g., area 000 or group 00 or serial 0000 are invalid; historically 666 and some 900–999 ranges were not used for ordinary SSNs).

Security note: don’t share your full SSN publicly. If you think your SSN has been exposed or misused, contact the SSA and consider placing a fraud alert or credit freeze with the major credit bureaus.

