Quick note — you wrote two different sets of digits (XXX-XX-2345 and “last four: 1234”). Which is the correct last-four if you want me to refer to it? I’ll answer your main question now.

What the middle two digits mean
- The middle two digits of an SSN are called the “group number.” The SSN format is AAA‑GG‑SSSS (area‑group‑serial).
- The group number is an internal bookkeeping element the Social Security Administration used to break an area (the first three digits) into smaller blocks when issuing numbers. Group numbers run from 01 through 99 (00 is not used).
- The group number itself does not encode personal information about you (no medical, tax, or other status), and it’s not a secret code about you.

Historical vs. current meaning
- Historically (before 2011), the first three digits (area number) were tied to the state or location where the SSN was issued, and the group number helped the SSA manage issuance. You could sometimes use area+group to estimate issuance patterns.
- Since June 25, 2011, SSA moved to “randomization.” Now the numbers (area and group) are assigned randomly, so the group number carries no geographic or issuance-date meaning anymore.

Other practical notes
- The last four digits (serial) are simply the individual serial within that group.
- The SSN’s parts are administrative; none of the parts are supposed to reveal protected personal attributes.
- Be careful about sharing your full SSN. Many forms ask only for the last four — provide the full SSN only to trusted, secure recipients.

If you want, tell me the exact middle digits you have (or confirm the last four), and I can explain whether, historically, that combination would have suggested anything about where or when it was issued — keeping in mind that after 2011 this is no longer meaningful.

