Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
U.S. Social Security numbers use the pattern AAA-GG-SSSS. The three parts are:

- AAA = area number  
- GG = group number (the middle two digits)  
- SSSS = serial number (last four digits)

What the middle (group) number means
- The two-digit GG is called the group number. It ranges from 01 to 99.  
- Historically the SSA used the group number together with the area number to organize and sequence the issuance of SSNs within an area — it was an administrative ordering code, not a code about you personally.  
- It is not a checksum and does not encode your birth date, gender, or other personal details.  
- Since SSA’s randomization policy (implemented in 2011), area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate geographic issuance; for SSNs issued after that change they have no geographic significance.

Security note
- The group number itself isn’t sensitive by itself, but your full SSN is sensitive. Be cautious about sharing the complete SSN and only give it to trusted parties when required.

If you want, tell me whether your SSN was issued before or after 2011 and I can say a bit more about what (if anything) the area/group might have meant historically.

