Short answer: the middle two digits are the SSN’s "group number." Historically they were an administrative code SSA used for issuing numbers; since 2011 they no longer carry meaningful geographic or personal information because SSA now randomizes SSNs.

Details:
- SSN format = AAA‑GG‑SSSS
  - AAA = area number (originally tied to the issuing office/state)
  - GG = group number (the two digits in the middle)
  - SSSS = serial number (last four)
- Historically (pre‑2011):
  - The group number helped SSA organize and sequence issuance within an area; it did not encode your birth date, age, or other personal details.
  - Group numbers were issued in a particular nonconsecutive order (01–99) to make number allocation easier.
- Since June 25, 2011 (randomization):
  - SSA stopped using area/group assignments to identify location or issuance order. Area and group numbers are now effectively random and do not reveal anything about where or when the SSN was issued.
- Validity notes:
  - Group "00" is not valid. Also, area "000", area "666", and area numbers 900–999 are not valid; serial "0000" is not valid.
- Privacy reminder:
  - Don’t share your full SSN publicly. You’re fine describing just the last four when necessary.

If you want, tell me the specific two middle digits you’re asking about (no need to post the whole SSN) and I can confirm whether that group number is one of the historically valid ranges — though it still won’t reveal any personal info beyond issuance bookkeeping. If you need an official verification or have concerns (lost/stolen SSN), contact the Social Security Administration.

