Short answer: those two middle digits are called the "group number." They were used by the Social Security Administration for internal filing/issuing purposes, but they don’t encode personal facts about you (like birthdate, sex, citizenship, etc.).

Details:
- SSNs are formatted AAA–GG–SSSS:
  - AAA = area number (originally tied to the state where the number was issued),
  - GG = group number (the middle two digits),
  - SSSS = serial number (the last four).
- Historically the group number helped the SSA organize and issue SSNs within an area number (it was an administrative ordering code, not personal data about you).
- Since SSN randomization in 2011, the area and group numbers no longer convey geographic or issuance-order information for newly issued numbers. Today the whole number is effectively randomized, so the group digits have no meaningful external interpretation.
- Importantly: the group digits do NOT indicate your age, gender, income, or any other personal trait.

Privacy note: avoid sharing your full SSN publicly. It’s usually safe to provide last four digits when required by a trusted service, but keep the full number private.

If you want, I can point you to the SSA’s page on SSN randomization or answer any follow-up questions.

