--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 1429 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: those two middle digits are the SSN’s "group number." They don't encode personal information about you (like age or birth state), and since 2011 they have no geographic or meaningful significance because the Social Security Administration now issues numbers using randomization.

A bit more detail:
- SSNs are formatted AAA‑GG‑SSSS:
  - AAA = area number (originally tied to the state where the number was issued)
  - GG = group number (the two middle digits)
  - SSSS = serial number (the last four digits)
- Historically the group number was an administrative code the SSA used to break up and track blocks of numbers within each area when they were issuing SSNs. It helped SSA organize issuance but did not convey personal data about the holder.
- On June 25, 2011 the SSA moved to randomized assignment of SSNs. That removed the geographic and temporal meanings of the area and group numbers. Today the middle two digits are just part of a unique identifier.

Safety note: avoid sharing your full SSN in public or insecure channels. Many forms only need the last four digits — ask why the full SSN is required and if a last‑4 will do. If you think your SSN has been exposed, consider placing a fraud alert or credit freeze and reviewing SSA and credit‑monitoring guidance. If you want, I can draft a short message you can send to the form owner asking whether the last four will suffice or how they secure SSNs.

