Quick safety note: it’s safer not to share your full SSN in places that aren’t secure or public. You typed two different “last four” values (2345 and 1234) — I won’t repeat either one here.

Short answer
- The two middle digits of a U.S. Social Security number are called the “group number.” They don’t encode personal details (no birthdate, gender, race, etc.). They were used by the Social Security Administration for internal filing and issuance order.

Details and history
- SSN format is usually shown as AAA‑GG‑SSSS:
  - AAA = area number (originally indicated the issuing office/state)
  - GG = group number (the middle two digits)
  - SSSS = serial number (the final four digits)
- Historically the area and group numbers reflected how the SSA assigned numbers (area tied roughly to state/location and group used to break the area into smaller blocks for processing). That was an administrative scheme, not personal information.
- Since June 25, 2011, the SSA uses “randomization” for new SSNs, so area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate where or when a number was issued. In modern SSNs the middle digits have no meaningful public interpretation.

Security reminder
- The full SSN is sensitive. If a form only asks for the “last four,” that is less sensitive but still avoid posting it publicly.
